{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r68.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t**Change the wording first, then run D750’s $0 writer test once.** I agree with Claude’s classification of all eight E replies. The $42B and $35B forecasts use the same gap in opposite directions. The proposed check exposes both, but “across the plausible readings” can also sound as though a forecast must survive *every* plausible reading. Alex explicitly allowed publication when one side is more likely and the other remains possible. [Paper check:22](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAPER-CHECK-D751.md:22) · [D747:11575](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11575)\n     2\t\n     3\t1. **Serious — the eight E replies support the paper check.** All four runs supplied the same 15 passages. Management reaffirmed an expectation, described future deployments and identified a customer evaluating Vera. The benchmark tests establish that a working platform existed. Those facts leave open both how much was already selling and how much customers would buy in FY2028. [E passages:9](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/E/user.txt:9) · [Evaluation and benchmarks:33](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/E/user.txt:33)\n     4\t\n     5\t   | Stored reply | Independent reading |\n     6\t   |---|---|\n     7\t   | Third run, Codex, **$34B** | **Exposed.** It takes 85% of the projected $20B base, then doubles $17B. Expected adoption explains “most” but gives no reason to choose a shortfall large enough to put FY2028 below $40B. [Reply:178](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/codex.json:178) |\n     8\t   | Third run, Claude, **$37B** | **Boundary case.** It explains why broad adoption favours growth above the market’s roughly 50%, why competition tempers the doubling forecast, and what would take sales above $40B. Readers can reasonably differ on whether that evidence makes a miss *more likely*. Earlier readings accepted it, and the D747 reading split. [Reply:162](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/claude.json:162) · [Reading history:11607](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11607) |\n     9\t   | Targeted run, Codex, **$36B** | **Exposed.** Describing the 10% cut as less than half an average quarter translates it into time. It supplies no evidence that this much business will slip or go to a rival. [Reply:130](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json:130) |\n    10\t   | Targeted run, Claude, **$32.4B** | **Exposed.** Its sensitivity check varies Vera’s share while holding the 1.8× growth estimate fixed. Its own lower growth comparison gives $20B × 1.5 × 90% = $27B, in a different band. The passages also leave the supposed prior-generation share unsized. [Reply:162](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json:162) |\n    11\t   | D749, Codex, **can’t tell** | **Preserved.** Both the base and the increase are projections. The writer explains how a modest shortfall could change the band and identifies the missing shipment pace, priced orders or delivery schedule that could distinguish the outcomes. [Reply:68](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json:68) |\n    12\t   | D749, Claude, **$42B** | **Exposed.** “Already selling at volume” is inferred from reaffirmed guidance. The writer uses that premise to take the target in full. Showing that 2.0× to just under 2.5× lands in its chosen band leaves unanswered why sales are likely to reach 2.0×. The original support vote remains split. [Reply:1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json:1) · [Result:18](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND5-RESULT.md:18) |\n    13\t   | D750, Codex, **can’t tell** | **Preserved.** The writer explains why customer interest and rising budgets support demand while leaving purchases by the deadline open. It identifies missing shipments against the base, scheduled deliveries, units and realised prices. [Reply:83](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json:83) |\n    14\t   | D750, Claude, **$35B** | **Exposed.** It treats market growth as NVIDIA’s floor and “more than double” as its ceiling, then takes the midpoint. Neither endpoint bounds realised sales. Even its 1.48× market comparison gives $29.6B, below its chosen $30–40B band. Both adjudicators rejected the forecast. [Reply:1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json:1) · [Result:19](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND6-RESULT.md:19) |\n    15\t\n    16\t2. **Minor — the three S forecasts stand, with one correction to Claude’s summary.** The two Codex replies compare the same four buyers’ AI-infrastructure plan with the $700B cutoff. They explain why more than $30B would have to fall outside the relevant spending or year, and why memory purchases, capacity demand and the scope of the reported delays favour clearing the cutoff. D750 Codex expressly allows a lower outcome in its $692.9–737.6B range. D750 Claude makes a different argument for $708.1B. It compares the deduction needed at the midpoint and top of total spending with Meta’s AI spending, Amazon’s memory purchases and raised budgets. The paper’s “$30–45B outside data centers” is too uniform a description of the three answers. All three earned settled **forecast support**. D750 Claude’s total-capex reference and its 104% ratio endpoint still have separate split defect votes. [D749 Codex:93](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/S/answers/codex.json:93) · [D750 Codex:29](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/S/answers/codex.json:29) · [D750 Claude:1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/S/answers/claude.json:1) · [Defect votes:24](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND6-RESULT.md:24)\n    17\t\n    18\t3. **Wording — change one sentence in line 9.** The exact sentence to replace is **“Before sizing a share, deduction, discount or pace that decides the band, explain which band the evidence favours.”** It is the third sentence on the line. Keep the existing routes for a supported size, a justifying bound and an explained comparison when the passages lack the quantity asked. Keep the existing instruction to answer unresolved and name the unknown. That avoids saying it twice. [Current line 9](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt:9) · [D745:11513](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11513) · [D747:11582](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11582)\n    19\t\n    20\t   - **Guidance alone.** “When other evidence bears on the forecast” preserves the guidance-alone rule. A repeat adds no new fact. When relevant evidence does arrive, the writer weighs it together with guidance and can still reach the same band. [System text:4](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt:4)\n    21\t   - **Not yet shipping.** Bullet 2 makes a discount conditional on both shipment status and evidential support. Bullet 3 says to weigh time and execution. On E, a customer evaluation cannot establish that commercial shipments have started or that they have yet to start. The final check should catch an assumption in either direction before the writer keeps or cuts the target. [System text:4](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt:4) · [Writer’s account:13](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/claude-r67.md:13)\n    22\t   - **“Not established” and missing confirmation.** The first concerns confidence that an outcome *already holds*. The second prevents missing confirmation from establishing the opposite event or an extreme band. Neither requires a move. A specific unresolved answer explains why the missing fact changes which band to publish. An explained forecast can acknowledge the same uncertainty and still favour one band. [System text:1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt:1) · [System text:5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt:5) · [D749:11645](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11645)\n    23\t   - **Smallest change.** Use “whether” to let the writer find that neither band is favoured. Ask it to *weigh* plausible alternatives so a range may cross a cutoff. This applies equally to sales, a spending share or a growth rate. The proposed full text grows from 4,867 to 4,976 characters, or 975 to 993 `o200k_base` tokens. The added words cover the previously implicit choice to take a target in full. [Existing request size:523](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:523)\n    24\t\n    25\t4. **Registration — reuse D750’s test and scoring, with round 67’s exit rule.** Pin the changed request before sending. Use one fresh writer from each family on E, S and P-v8, six replies total. Keep the invented-cut and impossible-floor controls with their original requests. Retain blinded readers, adjudication, and the requirement to record the decisive premise and its passage support. A clean pass requires all six replies to be publishable, settled support on both E and S pairs, supported **forecasts** on both P-v8 replies, clear defect votes and both controls read as registered. E and S may each earn support for a specific unresolved answer, so a pass alone cannot establish how often the writer will publish a forecast. [Cases and controls:463](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:463) · [Pass and fail conditions:480](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:480) · [D750 premise checks:537](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:537)\n    26\t\n    27\t**Verdict.** Revise line 9 as below, then run the one registered writer test. A clean pass goes to Alex for paid authorization. A settled failure or persistent split ends the wording loop and returns the publication choice to Alex. This review used stored replies only. No file changed and no request was sent. [Round 67 exit rule:13](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r67.md:13)\n    28\t\n    29\t**Exact final text of line 9**\n    30\t\n    31\t```text\n    32\t- Return the family's figures and one answer per member, as the JSON schema defines them. Every number an answer rests on is a figure; a number you judge rather than read or compute is an estimate, stated as yours. When other evidence bears on the forecast, weigh the plausible alternatives for any unknown fact that decides the band. Explain whether the evidence as a whole favours one band before deciding whether or how much to adjust. Show support for the size or a justifying bound. If the passages lack the quantity asked, explain why a related figure fits the companies, period and quantity, and why your assumptions favour that band. If you can say only that the outcome might fall short or overshoot, not which band is more likely, answer unresolved and name what is unknown. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n    33\t```","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND5-RESULT.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# The D749 writer test of the E writer repair: result\n     2\t\n     3\tRun folder: `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/`. It was registered in `SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D749], with council round 64's\n     4\tchanges, and scored by `blind5.py tally` (`TALLY.json`, `reasons.json`). The request is `judging-v13s.txt`, sha256 `ac707fa9…`.\n     5\t\n     6\t## 1. Verdict: INCONCLUSIVE (two support splits, X05 and X08); no line failed\n     7\t\n     8\t**A correction, made after council round 65 (`codex-r65.md`).** I first recorded X08's two \"yes\" readings as compatible, although\n     9\tmy own note named the tension. The Claude reader read e1947 as a floor (\"at least $730B\") while accepting $710.4B. The Codex reader\n    10\tread it as a guide. Under the registered procedure, matching votes with incompatible reasons go to adjudication.\n    11\t- X08 was sent to adjudication. Both adjudicators said yes. The Claude adjudicator again read a floor above the outcome, and the\n    12\t  Codex adjudicator did not reconcile the $730B figure.\n    13\t- X08 is recorded as split (`reasons.json`). The row below is corrected, and the verdict stays INCONCLUSIVE.\n    14\t\n    15\t| Packet | Answer | Shape, rung | First readers (Codex, Claude) | Adjudicators (Codex, Claude) | Settled | Line |\n    16\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n    17\t| X06 | fresh E Codex | unresolved | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    18\t| X05 | fresh E Claude | forecast, rung 2 ($42B: 2.1x, 100% Vera) | no, yes | no, yes | split | inconclusive |\n    19\t| X02 | fresh S Codex | forecast, rung 4 ($730B estimate from the same four companies' AI-infrastructure plan) | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    20\t| X08 | fresh S Claude | forecast, rung 4 ($740B × 96% = $710.4B) | yes, yes (reasons incompatible: floor against guide) | yes, yes (reasons incompatible again) | split | inconclusive |\n    21\t| X01 | fresh P-v8 Claude | forecast, rung 2 ($383.9B) | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    22\t| X04 | fresh P-v8 Codex | forecast, rung 2 ($380.8B) | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    23\t| X03 | control: invented 10% cut (blind4 U05) | forecast | no, no | (none) | no | ok |\n    24\t| X07 | control: impossible floor (blind4 U02) | forecast | floor marked by Codex only | floor upheld by both | floor settled | ok |\n    25\t\n    26\t- **The code** accepted all six fresh replies, and all six are publishable.\n    27\t- **No defect** was marked on a fresh reply.\n    28\t- **The reasons** behind the five settled fresh pairs and the two controls were read by Claude and recorded as compatible\n    29\t  (`reasons.json`). X08 is corrected to incompatible, as above.\n    30\t\n    31\t## 2. What changed against the targeted test (D745), same cases\n    32\t\n    33\t- **E.** The Codex writer no longer invents a cut. It answers unresolved and names the gap: both the FY2027 base and the FY2028\n    34\t  increase are projections, and no passage gives a shipment run-rate, an order with units and prices, or a deployment schedule. Both\n    35\t  readers accept it. In the targeted test, the same writer type cut the reference by 10% and was settled not supported.\n    36\t- **S.** Both fresh forecasts are supported by both families. In the targeted test both split.\n    37\t- **P-v8.** Both fresh replies are supported forecasts, as before. The repair did not cause withholding on the supported case.\n    38\t- **The controls.** Both read as expected.\n    39\t\n    40\t## 3. The one split, read against its passages (Claude's own trace reading)\n    41\t\n    42\t- **The answer.** E's Claude writer takes management's \"more than double\" in full, at 2.1x on $20B with Vera as 100%, giving $42B,\n    43\t  rung 2. It argues the FY2027 base \"is reaffirmed for the current year, so the CPU line is already selling at volume rather than a\n    44\t  product with no shipments\".\n    45\t- **The passages it saw (15).** None shows Vera shipments, reported Vera revenue or orders:\n    46\t  - e4689: \"will be deployed\";\n    47\t  - e5044: Anthropic \"is evaluating\";\n    48\t  - e4856: the $20B is a \"potential\";\n    49\t  - e3660: management \"still expects\" $20B, which is an outlook;\n    50\t  - the AMD benchmarks (e5399–e5404) show only that a Vera platform exists to be tested.\n    51\t- **The request's own rule** (bullet 2) says a target for a product with no shipments yet \"is taken in full only when the evidence\n    52\t  shows the volume in hand\".\n    53\t- **On the passages, the premise that decides this answer is not shown.**\n    54\t  - The Codex reader and the Codex adjudicator say so.\n    55\t  - The Claude adjudicator accepted the reaffirmed outlook and the benchmarked platform as enough.\n    56\t  - This is a question of fact against an existing rule, not a gap in Alex's D747 words. Where the families differ is whether a\n    57\t    reaffirmed outlook counts as \"volume in hand\".\n    58\t\n    59\t## 4. A trace note the readers did not mark (it became the X08 split after the correction)\n    60\t\n    61\t- **What the answer says.** S's Claude reply (X08) says data-center capex \"contains all AI capex plus non-AI cloud\n    62\t  capacity\". With e1947's more than $730B of AI infrastructure, that implies data-center capex above $730B, while its own outcome is\n    63\t  $710.4B.\n    64\t- **What it means.** The tension runs in the conservative direction and does not move the band (both figures are in rung 4).\n    65\t  Neither reader marked it. It is disclosed, not scored.\n    66\t\n    67\t## 5. Not done\n    68\t\n    69\tNothing was paid. Nothing was tuned on the 36 stored answers. The request was not changed after the registration.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"d=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers\nrg -n 'CY2027|CY2026|2027|2026|singleton|attain|ceiling|wrong year' \\\"\"'$d/BLIND6-RESULT.md\" \"$d/SMALL-DESIGN.md\" \"$d/paid_gate.py\" \"$d/round69-brief.md\" | rg '\"'CY2027|CY2026|2027|attain|wrong year' | cut -c1-400 | tail -110\nnl -ba \\\"\"'$d/BLIND6-RESULT.md\" | tail -65\nnl -ba \"$d/judging-v13s.txt\"\nnl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '\"'11640,11725p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"d=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers\nrg -n 'CY2027|CY2026|2027|2026|singleton|attain|ceiling|wrong year' \\\"\"'$d/BLIND6-RESULT.md\" \"$d/SMALL-DESIGN.md\" \"$d/paid_gate.py\" \"$d/round69-brief.md\" | rg '\"'CY2027|CY2026|2027|attain|wrong year' | cut -c1-400 | tail -110\nnl -ba \\\"\"'$d/BLIND6-RESULT.md\" | tail -65\nnl -ba \"$d/judging-v13s.txt\"\nnl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '\"'11640,11725p'\"","aggregated_output":"docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/round69-brief.md:11:- **The paid gate's lines** are in `PAID-GATE.md` §4. Its singleton S case asks about CY2027 (`paid_gate.py` SINGLETONS).\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/round69-brief.md:34:   the attainable verdict from `PAID-GATE.md` §4)? Or should the recommendation be different?\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:43:SINGLETONS = [('O', 'old-2', 'v29', 8734, ('NVIDIA', 'equity investment commitments', 'company', 'end of FY2027')),\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:44:              ('S', 'old-2', 'v6', 8595, ('four largest AI hyperscalers', 'data-center capex', 'combined', 'CY2027')),\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:67:    return {'members': {total[0]: {'what': W('NVIDIA', 'revenue', 'company', 'FY2027'), 'bands': bands(draw, total[0])},\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:68:                        dc[0]: {'what': W('NVIDIA', 'revenue', 'Data Center', 'FY2027'), 'bands': bands(draw, dc[0])}},\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:70:            'shared': [W('NVIDIA', 'revenue', 'company', 'FY2027 Q3'), W('NVIDIA', 'revenue', 'company', 'FY2027 Q4')],\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:71:            'compare_periods': ['FY2027 Q3', 'FY2027 Q4', 'FY2027']}\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:94:O_NOTE = 'the balance at the end of FY2027, as the FY2027 annual report publishes it'\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:45:| S8 | a part added into its whole; periods misadded | A sum never adds a figure that uses (or is) the part's quantity to one that uses (or is) the whole's: the whole already includes the part. A difference may subtract the part from the whole only to give the rest, another scope. When a sum's or difference's inputs are periods of one quan\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:66:| S8 | a part added into its whole; periods misadded | A sum or difference never combines the part's quantity with the whole's (Relations). When a sum's or difference's inputs are periods of one quantity written as FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1 or FY2027 Q1-Q3, they make up the output's period exactly (Q1 + Q2 = H1; FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4). | `ope\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:88:| `operation` | \"Computed only. sum(a, b, …) = a + b + … (one unit); difference(a, b) = a − b (one unit); product(a, b, …) = a × b × … (USD bn × x, x × x or % × x; never USD bn × %); ratio(a, b) = a ÷ b (same units give x; USD bn ÷ x gives USD bn); share(whole, percent) = whole × percent ÷ 100; growth(old, new) = the % change; apply_growth\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:98:- For O: \"<key>: the balance at the end of FY2027, as the FY2027 annual report publishes it.\"\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:108:| `what.period` | string | \"For example FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1, FY2027 Q1-Q3, CY2026, end of FY2027.\" |\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:135:- \"Relations: v8 is part of v4: for each of FY2027 Q3, FY2027 Q4, FY2027, the part's forecast figure may not exceed the whole's, and\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:168:| P (joint), P-v4, P-v8 (separate) | old-1 v4 8810, v8 8816 | NVIDIA revenue company FY2027; NVIDIA revenue Data Center FY2027 | 431.2/392/352.8/313.6; 400/350/300/250 | the pair; 79 quotes absent from the gate |\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:169:| S | old-3 v6 9043 | four largest AI hyperscalers, data-center capex, combined, CY2026 | 500/600/700/800 | fresh: 41 of 50 quotes absent from the gate |\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:170:| O | old-2 v29 8507 | NVIDIA equity investment commitments company end of FY2027 (the fiscal year-end balance the annual report publishes) | 25/50/75/100 | control |\n     1\t# The D750 second writer test: result\n     2\t\n     3\tRun folder: `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/`. It was registered in `SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D750], with council round 66's\n     4\tchanges, and scored by `blind6.py tally` (`TALLY.json`, `reasons.json`). The request is `judging-v13s.txt`, sha256 `54f5847d…`.\n     5\t\n     6\t## 1. Verdict: FAIL on one settled line\n     7\t\n     8\t| Packet | Answer | Shape, rung | First readers (Codex, Claude) | Adjudicators (Codex, Claude) | Settled | Line |\n     9\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n    10\t| X06 | fresh E Claude | forecast, rung 3 ($35B: 1.75x pace) | yes, no | no, no | **no** | **FAIL** |\n    11\t| X07 | fresh E Codex | unresolved | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    12\t| X05 | fresh S Codex | forecast, rung 4 ($692.9–737.6B, central about $715B) | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    13\t| X08 | fresh S Claude | forecast, rung 4 ($708.1B: $715–745B × 97%) | yes, yes; Codex marked two defects | defects: uphold, reject | yes; two defect splits | inconclusive |\n    14\t| X02 | fresh P-v8 Claude | forecast, rung 2 ($385.9B) | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    15\t| X04 | fresh P-v8 Codex | forecast, rung 2 ($377.7B) | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    16\t| X03 | control: invented 10% cut | forecast | no, no | (none) | no | ok |\n    17\t| X01 | control: impossible floor | forecast | floor marked by both | (support only) | floor settled | ok |\n    18\t\n    19\t- **The failure.** X06's writer took the market pace (about 1.48–1.5x) as a floor and management's \"more than double\" as a\n    20\t  ceiling, and chose 1.75x, the midpoint.\n    21\t  - Both adjudicators: the passages state \"more than double\" as a minimum; the midpoint has no source; the reasons for a miss\n    22\t    (one customer evaluating, a competitor's own benchmarks, missing volume figures) size or bound no shortfall below $40B. The\n    23\t    answer shows only that the outcome might fall short.\n    24\t- **The defect splits on X08.**\n    25\t  - F15 = 130 ÷ (125–145) gives a range whose top end is 104%. The Codex adjudicator calls that a part above its whole. The Claude\n    26\t    adjudicator calls it the mechanical end of a range ratio that the format requires.\n    27\t  - F5, the outside total-capex range, is marked as the market's reference for data-center spending. The Codex adjudicator says this\n    28\t    implies a 100% share. The Claude adjudicator says F5 is a quoted outside figure, as the reference rule allows, and that the\n    29\t    deduction is set out separately.\n    30\t- **The reasons and premises** behind every settled S and E vote were read and recorded (`reasons.json`). X05 and X08's readers read\n    31\t  the same four companies' AI-infrastructure plan as a plan that can slip, not as a floor, which fits each answer's own outcome.\n    32\t- **A run incident, disclosed.** The background command running the eight Codex readers was terminated (exit 144) while three\n    33\t  readers were still running. X04, X05 and X08 had empty outputs, kept in `reader-packets/interrupted/`. Fresh Codex readers were\n    34\t  run for those three. No reading was discarded after it was seen.\n    35\t\n    36\t## 2. The E record across the four runs (same case, same passages)\n    37\t\n    38\t| Run | Codex writer | Claude writer |\n    39\t|---|---|---|\n    40\t| third run (D742 request) | 85% realisation, $34B; settled no | 1.85x bracket, $37B; yes in re-read 2, split under D747 |\n    41\t| targeted test (D745) | 90% realisation, $36B; settled no | 1.8x × 90% share, $32.4B; split, then no under D747 |\n    42\t| D749 | unresolved, specific gap; settled yes | 2.1x in full, $42B, asserting \"already selling at volume\"; split |\n    43\t| D750 | unresolved, specific gap; settled yes | 1.75x bracket midpoint, $35B; settled no |\n    44\t\n    45\t- **The Codex writer.** The repair (D749, D750) moved it from invented cuts to a justified unresolved, twice.\n    46\t- **The Claude writer.** It forecasts every time, by a different route each time, and has never been settled supported under the\n    47\t  current definitions.\n    48\t- **S** improved but did not fully settle (corrected after council 67):\n    49\t  - D750's two fresh forecasts earned settled support, with two defect marks on X08 still split.\n    50\t  - D749's X08 support verdict was split after the correction: one reader accepted $710.4B while treating $730B as a floor.\n    51\t  - D749's S Codex was settled supported.\n    52\t- **P-v8** has been supported throughout.\n    53\t\n    54\t## 3. Not done\n    55\t\n    56\tNothing was paid. Nothing was tuned on the 36 stored answers. The request was not changed after the registration.\n     1\tYou are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging the current rungs of a family of related number theses: one company's quantities that overlap, such as a total and its segment, a level and its growth, or a later year that rests on an earlier year's base. Use only the supplied evidence and themes in force. Each rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n     2\t\n     3\t- Judge every member from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently) and the themes in force, as if for the first time. Earlier forecasts are not shown: whether a conclusion changed is compared afterwards.\n     4\t- Judge each member against a reference: what the market expects for that question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on its ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate whose pace you forecast, informed by guidance, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted subject to the evidence requirement below; it remains a forecast when reaffirmed, and is taken in full only when you show what evidence puts the required volume in hand for the period. Build the family's figures once and read every member's outcome from them: a part cannot exceed its whole, a figure that spans several periods or products gives a question only its share, and a quantity the family shares has one forecast. Compare each outcome with its rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n     5\t- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input, not an answer: weigh time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say) under the evidence requirement below; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n     6\t- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n     7\t- \"Themes in force\" lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on a member and report it in that member's theme_pushes with the direction it pushes that member (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \"updated since this thesis was last judged\" may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n     8\t- Your conclusions must be consistent with each other and with the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open.\n     9\t- Return the family's figures and one answer per member, as the JSON schema defines them. Every number an answer rests on is a figure; a number you judge rather than read or compute is an estimate, stated as yours. When other evidence bears on the forecast, weigh the plausible alternatives for any unknown fact that decides the band. Explain whether the evidence as a whole favours one band before deciding whether or how much to adjust. Show support for the size or a justifying bound. If the passages lack the quantity asked, explain why a related figure fits the companies, period and quantity, and why your assumptions favour that band. If you can say only that the outcome might fall short or overshoot, not which band is more likely, answer unresolved and name what is unknown. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n 11640\t\n 11641\t## D749 · 2026-11-23 · Alex authorizes the E writer repair and its $0 writer test\n 11642\t\n 11643\t- **The question** (after D748): \"下一步怎么走？\"\n 11644\t  - Options:\n 11645\t    - \"批准修写手再小测 (推荐)\": the writer first decides whether the evidence favours one side of the line, then whether to discount;\n 11646\t      if it can only say sales might fall short, it answers \"can't tell\" and says what is missing. The changed request goes to the\n 11647\t      council, then one $0 writer test on E, S and P with the two failed answers as controls. A clean pass comes back to Alex for the\n 11648\t      paid re-send.\n 11649\t    - \"不修，直接议花钱重发\".\n 11650\t    - \"先停在这里\".\n 11651\t- **Alex's answer** (2026-11-23): \"批准修写手再小测 (推荐)\".\n 11652\t- **Decided (Alex).** Repair the writer request as council 63 described. Review it in council before any send. Then run one $0\n 11653\t  blind writer test on E, S and P-v8, with the saved U05 (invented cut) and U02 (impossible floor) as reader controls, under D748's\n 11654\t  pass lines. The paid re-send stays on hold until that test passes clean.\n 11655\t\n 11656\t## D750 · 2026-11-23 · The D749 writer test is INCONCLUSIVE with no failed line; council 65 (under D439): one bullet-2 sentence on reaffirmed targets, then a second $0 writer test\n 11657\t\n 11658\t- **Alex's direction** (2026-11-23, before sleeping): \"你接下来开始自行收敛，方向吧，我要准备睡了，所以这边的决策交给你，还有 Council， 记得不要 Scope Drift， 我们的目标是把剩下有问题的地方收敛起来，然后准备要往 Production 方向去走了，所以你自己每次复习的时候看 Log， 看我们试过了哪些东西，不要犯一模一样的错误，然后赶紧的往收敛方向去做测量和把它定下来。\"\n 11659\t  - D439 governs: the council decides reversibly, no paid send (its ceiling is spent), nothing committed.\n 11660\t- **The result** (`docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND5-RESULT.md`; run `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/`):\n 11661\t  - **The repair worked where it was aimed.**\n 11662\t    - E's Codex writer, which invented a cut in both earlier runs, now answers a justified unresolved, accepted by both families.\n 11663\t    - Both P-v8 replies are supported forecasts.\n 11664\t    - Both controls were caught: the invented cut settled no, and the impossible floor was upheld by both adjudicators.\n 11665\t    - No fresh defect, no refusal. S's Codex forecast is supported.\n 11666\t  - **Two splits:**\n 11667\t    - X05, E Claude, takes a not-yet-shipping target in full, asserting \"already selling at volume\". No passage shows shipments,\n 11668\t      revenue to date or orders.\n 11669\t    - X08, S Claude: one reader cites \"more than $730B\" as a floor above its own $710.4B outcome, the other reads it as a guide.\n 11670\t- **Claude's error, corrected.** I first recorded X08's reasons as compatible although my own note named the tension. Council 65\n 11671\t  caught it. X08 was then adjudicated as registered and stayed split.\n 11672\t- **Council round 65** (`claude-r65.md`, `codex-r65.md`) reached consensus:\n 11673\t  - X05's premise is a question of fact under the existing rule, not a gap in Alex's words.\n 11674\t  - Claude's closed list of \"volume in hand\" was rejected: it was wider and narrower than the standard at once.\n 11675\t  - The smaller sentence is at bullet 2: \"it remains a forecast when reaffirmed, and is taken in full only when you show what evidence\n 11676\t    puts the required volume in hand for the period\".\n 11677\t  - One $0 writer test on all three cases (Alex's three-case condition), with the same controls and lines. On S, a reading that cites a\n 11678\t    floor above the answer's own outcome is not compatible with one that reads a guide.\n 11679\t  - The paid re-send stays on hold.\n 11680\t- **Done.** The sentence was changed (4,794 → 4,867 characters). Cases 111 of 111, rehearsal 19 of 19, pinned plan unchanged. The test\n 11681\t  is registered (`SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D750]) and built by `blind6.py`. Council round 66 reviews it before any send.\n 11682\t\n 11683\t## D751 · 2026-11-23 · D750 fails on E's bracket midpoint; council 67–68 (under D439): one structural line-9 change, a paper check first, then one last writer test with an exit rule back to Alex\n 11684\t\n 11685\t- **The D750 result** (`docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND6-RESULT.md`): FAIL on one settled line.\n 11686\t  - X06, E Claude, $35B: it bracketed the pace between the market pace and \"more than double\", treated as a ceiling, and took the\n 11687\t    midpoint. Both adjudicators said not supported, with compatible reasons.\n 11688\t  - Passed:\n 11689\t    - E Codex: a justified unresolved, again;\n 11690\t    - both S forecasts, supported;\n 11691\t    - both P-v8 forecasts, supported;\n 11692\t    - both controls, caught.\n 11693\t  - Split: two defect marks on S Claude (a 104% range-ratio end; a total-capex range marked as the data-center reference).\n 11694\t  - A run incident, disclosed: three Codex readers were interrupted (exit 144) before writing and were rerun fresh.\n 11695\t- **The writer's own account** (asked after the fact). Bullets 2 and 3 (the not-yet-shipping discount) and \"'not established' … never\n 11696\t  a reason to stay\" pushed it to a cut. It rejected the unresolved sentence because it took the market paths as a bound, and it now\n 11697\t  agrees they are not one. It noted that the passages never say whether Vera is shipping.\n 11698\t- **Council round 67** (`claude-r67.md`, `codex-r67.md`):\n 11699\t  - Claude's lean was to stop the $0 loop and ask Alex to waive D749's clean-pass condition for the paid re-send.\n 11700\t  - Codex showed that would waive Alex's own condition while he is asleep, and named the one structural mistake: an unknown fact\n 11701\t    decides the side of the line, and the writers assert it in either direction ($42B, $35B).\n 11702\t  - Consensus: one change to the final evidence check, a paper check on all eight stored E replies first, then one $0 writer test.\n 11703\t  - The exit rule: a settled failure or persistent split ends the wording loop and returns the publication choice to Alex.\n 11704\t- **Council round 68** (`codex-r68.md`) verified Claude's paper check (`PAPER-CHECK-D751.md`) row by row:\n 11705\t  - the $42B and $35B premises are exposed;\n 11706\t  - both specific \"can't tell\" answers are preserved;\n 11707\t  - $37B stays a boundary case;\n 11708\t  - the explained near-cutoff S forecasts stand.\n 11709\t\n 11710\t  It replaced \"across the plausible readings\", which was stricter than D747, with: \"When other evidence bears on the forecast, weigh\n 11711\t  the plausible alternatives for any unknown fact that decides the band. Explain whether the evidence as a whole favours one band\n 11712\t  before deciding whether or how much to adjust.\"\n 11713\t- **Done:**\n 11714\t  - the line-9 sentence replaced (4,867 → 4,976 characters, 975 → 993 tokens); cases 111 of 111, rehearsal 19 of 19, pinned plan\n 11715\t    unchanged;\n 11716\t  - the test registered (`SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D751]) and built by `blind7.py`: D750's design and tally, with the exit rule.\n 11717\t- **Open defect, recorded, not addressed by this change:** a total-capex range marked as the market's reference for data-center\n 11718\t  spending. Council 67 would uphold it under D741 ruling 2's \"an outside figure for the member's own quantity\". The reader definition\n 11719\t  of that defect type covers only the writer's own estimates. It is a separate class.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"d=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers\nrg -n -i 'attain|best possible|maximum|at most [56] of 9|3 more|gain|separate arm|cannot pass|can still pass|ceiling on' \\\"\"'$d\"/{SMALL-DESIGN.md,RESULT.md,PAID-GATE-RESULT.md,round69-brief.md,BLIND-RESULT.md,BLIND7-RESULT.md,BLIND3-RESULT.md}\nnl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '\"'10957,11007p;11509,11516p;11558,11590p'\nnl -ba \\\"\"'$d/SMALL-DESIGN.md\" | tail -36\nnl -ba \"$d/paid_gate.py\" | sed -n '\"'34,50p'\nrg -n 'usable|deciding_gap' \\\"\"'$d/paid_gate.py\" | tail -35'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"d=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers\nrg -n -i 'attain|best possible|maximum|at most [56] of 9|3 more|gain|separate arm|cannot pass|can still pass|ceiling on' \\\"\"'$d\"/{SMALL-DESIGN.md,RESULT.md,PAID-GATE-RESULT.md,round69-brief.md,BLIND-RESULT.md,BLIND7-RESULT.md,BLIND3-RESULT.md}\nnl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '\"'10957,11007p;11509,11516p;11558,11590p'\nnl -ba \\\"\"'$d/SMALL-DESIGN.md\" | tail -36\nnl -ba \"$d/paid_gate.py\" | sed -n '\"'34,50p'\nrg -n 'usable|deciding_gap' \\\"\"'$d/paid_gate.py\" | tail -35'","aggregated_output":"docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md:47:  - e1947: more than $730B \"on AI infrastructure\" against $720–745B of capex;\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md:53:- **R06 (O, Claude writer) is split again** on the $18B reading: Codex says not supported, Claude says supported. This is the same\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md:69:  - R11's prose offers \"$138 billion in Q4\" as reaching rung 1, but it gives $428.32B against the $431.2B edge. The forecast itself\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md:84:- **STOP again, as written: Q10 (P-v8, Codex writer).**\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md:99:  - O's Claude reply (Q09) is split again.\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md:127:  - O's Codex reply is supported after adjudication; O's Claude reply is split again.\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/round69-brief.md:34:   the attainable verdict from `PAID-GATE.md` §4)? Or should the recommendation be different?\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:23:| bounds and their propagation (C2 bound direction, C3/C6 open ends) | a source's qualifier is checked against the quote and shown to readers; computed figures are points or ranges |\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:152:  than today's (17 figure fields against 23, shorter descriptions than v2).\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:253:- **INCOMPLETE** is judged against the inventory of 12 first replies and 24 readings plus the third readings due.\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:260:  answer, accepted or refused, against the shown passages. For each answer they record:\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:329:  1. check quotes and calculations against the format rules;\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:341:  | K-unsupported | E's Claude reply, 140% growth that its own reasons argue against | supported = no |\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:444:  `ac707fa9…`, checked by `blind5.py build` against every built system text). This is the wording after council round 64\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:518:  checked by `blind6.py build` against every built system text). Bullet 2's \"it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:538:  `blind5.py`, shared by `blind6.py`, gains three changes:\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND-RESULT.md:40:of the writer or the checker. Where the two disagreed, two adjudicators, one per family, settled each point against the passages and\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND-RESULT.md:70:  - The Claude adjudicator calls it supported: the passages use the two terms interchangeably (e5376 against e5081).\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RESULT.md:22:  - old-2 at 09-20: Data Center Q4 is about $146B, the whole company's Q4 about $127B. In old-1 on 09-20 it is $126B against $112.2B.\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RESULT.md:25:    and 09-12: $419B, $422B and $414B against $407B.\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RESULT.md:29:  cite none in common. Across draws, total against CPU shares 0–5%. The SemiAnalysis uplift was filed under Data Center only.\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RESULT.md:88:    format and checks. Plus three samples each on the O, S and E requests. Every output is read against its source with the arm\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RESULT.md:91:    and at least 3 more usable pairs than the separate arm. Zero published hard contradictions, internal-as-market references, or\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE-RESULT.md:4:was published, and the joint arm is usable in 0 of 9 pairs against the registered 8 of 9.\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE-RESULT.md:92:  against the check.\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE-RESULT.md:118:A clean result earns a request to Alex to send the same 36-request comparison again under a new decision, with D725's lines\n 10957\t## D725 · 2026-11-22 · Council round 45/45b: the number answers fail because related numbers have no shared, checked calculation; one judge version and one test recommended to Alex\n 10958\t\n 10959\t- **Alex's words** (2026-11-22): \"work with counsel and have a look at the traces of this problem … what is wrong with the system design\n 10960\t  overall … look at the code, look at the traces, deeply battle it out with counsel … because I think it's the most important thing\n 10961\t  that we don't hallucinate with the numbers, and I want you to get this right\".\n 10962\t- **The record:** `docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/` (`RESULT.md`).\n 10963\t  - Claude's reflection was written before Codex's.\n 10964\t  - Codex ran an independent investigation (fresh thread `01a20501…`) and a challenge round.\n 10965\t  - Consensus. One wording point stays open: the $146B is arithmetically a residual, but the order of the judge's choices is unknown.\n 10966\t- **Diagnosis:**\n 10967\t  1. Answers are prose; code ties no figure to a source and recomputes nothing. The production check passes contradictory or invented\n 10968\t     figures.\n 10969\t  2. Related questions compute the same quantities separately. The unit is one question, with 0 dependencies.\n 10970\t  3. Filing and the evidence in view split their inputs. The previous conclusion carries figures without provenance.\n 10971\t  4. Opening's bands amplify an error.\n 10972\t- **Measured:**\n 10973\t  - Data Center Q4 above company Q4 on at least 13 of 26 days in old-1;\n 10974\t  - Data Center's full year above the total's on 20 consecutive days in old-2;\n 10975\t  - different Q3 bases on 17 of 18 paired days;\n 10976\t  - shared evidence between total and Data Center 8% in old-2;\n 10977\t  - 17 of the 24 double-rejected answers from unchecked arithmetic.\n 10978\t- **Recommended design** (a new judge version, Alex's to open):\n 10979\t  - one judging call per related family, each question keeping its answer from one calculation;\n 10980\t  - figures labelled by the judge (source, calculation, assumption; scope and period);\n 10981\t  - code checks only identity and arithmetic (figure in quote, recompute, part within whole, one value per shared quantity, a sourced\n 10982\t    reference);\n 10983\t  - unreconciled forecasts shown as unresolved;\n 10984\t  - extraction unchanged;\n 10985\t  - families declared for the test.\n 10986\t- **Interim for a first release:** number questions shown as \"Forecast unavailable\" until they pass the checks.\n 10987\t- **The test:** a $0 gate (hand reconstructions of five stored cases, four gold controls, three planted errors), then a paid gate\n 10988\t  (36 calls; joint against separate on the three draws' 09-20 pair, plus O, S and E singletons). Pass and stop lines are in `RESULT.md`.\n 10989\t- **Not done:** nothing built, sent or changed.\n 10990\t\n 10991\t## D726 · 2026-11-22 · Alex opens a judge version for related number questions ($0 gate first) and agrees to \"Forecast unavailable\" as the first-release interim\n 10992\t\n 10993\t- **The questions** (after D725):\n 10994\t  - \"要不要开一个新的判官版本来试修法（相关的题合在一次里算，每个数字标明出处，程序检查算术和出处）？\" Options: 开，先做 0 元那一关\n 10995\t    (recommended: the format and checks designed and through the council, then the $0 gate on five stored cases, four gold controls and\n 10996\t    three planted errors; the paid gate asks Alex for its ceiling); 先不开.\n 10997\t  - \"第一版上线前的过渡：数字题只显示问题和来源，档位和预测文字写成'预测暂缺'，等通过检查再放出来。你同意这个做法吗？\" Options: 同意\n 10998\t    (recommended); 暂不定.\n 10999\t- **Alex's answers** (2026-11-22): \"开，先做 0 元那一关 (推荐)\"; \"同意 (推荐)\".\n 11000\t- **Decided (Alex):**\n 11001\t  - A new judge version is opened for the bounded test of D725: related number questions judged together, with labelled figures and\n 11002\t    code checks of identity and arithmetic. It is designed and reviewed at $0, then the $0 gate runs. The paid gate needs Alex's\n 11003\t    ceiling.\n 11004\t  - Until the number answers pass, a first release shows number questions with their sources and \"Forecast unavailable\": the band, the\n 11005\t    forecast text and its history withheld together. It is built with the release work, which is not started.\n 11006\t- poc-v118 stays the product.\n 11007\t\n 11509\t  - Falsifier: a fresh S answer still picks a band on an unsupported share, or the supported case is withheld.\n 11510\t- **Alex's answers** (2026-11-23):\n 11511\t  - to \"怎么走？\": \"批准针对性小测 (推荐)\". The other options were \"不小测，直接议花钱重发\" and \"先停在这里\".\n 11512\t  - to whether a justified \"无法判断\" counts: \"算合格 (推荐)\". The other option was \"不算\".\n 11513\t- **Decided (Alex):**\n 11514\t  1. Run the targeted $0 test.\n 11515\t  2. An unresolved answer whose reason shows the passages cannot settle the rung counts as a usable answer.\n 11516\t- **Council round 60** (`codex-r60.md`) set the wording and the registration (`SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D745]):\n 11558\t    comparison applies to the companies, the period and the quantity asked, and why, under its own assumptions, that side of the line\n 11559\t    is more likely.\n 11560\t  - **The one test after his ruling.** A $0 blinded re-read of nine stored answers under his words, added to the shared definitions:\n 11561\t    - the answers: U01–U06, U08, the third run's T10 (for its spending argument only), and T15 (the accepted E bracket);\n 11562\t    - fresh readers, one per family;\n 11563\t    - what his choice implies for each answer is recorded before reading.\n 11564\t    - It moves on to a writer repair only if both families apply his words alike. A continuing split leaves the publication question\n 11565\t      open in practice. A clean re-read does not turn D745 into a pass.\n 11566\t  - **The paid re-send stays on hold** until an E writer repair passes a clean $0 test. The proposed repair: the writer chooses between\n 11567\t    a forecast and \"can't tell\" before it assigns a discount, naming what makes one side more likely under its own assumptions. It is a\n 11568\t    new arm and goes to council before any send. Publishing management's figure whenever it sits on a cutoff would change the product,\n 11569\t    and needs Alex's own decision.\n 11570\t- **The question to Alex** (pending): \"资料没直接给出所问的数。用一个相关数字作比较，能解释为什么结果更可能在线的这一边，但另一边仍有可能。这时可以发布预测，还是要等到能从资料算出结果落在线的哪一边？\"\n 11571\t- **Not done.** Nothing was paid. Nothing was tuned on the 36 stored answers. The request was not changed after the registration.\n 11572\t\n 11573\t## D747 · 2026-11-23 · Alex rules that a forecast may rest on an explained indirect comparison\n 11574\t\n 11575\t- **The question** (after D746): \"资料没直接给出题目问的那个数，只能拿一个相关的数字来比。比如问四大云厂商今年的数据中心开支能不能过 7000 亿，资料只说“这四家 AI 基础设施开支超过 7300 亿”。这种比较能说明结果更可能落在线的某一边，但另一边也还有可能。这时系统可以发布预测吗，还是要等资料能直接算出落在哪边？\"\n 11576\t  - Options:\n 11577\t    - \"可以发布，要讲清楚 (推荐)\": the answer must explain why the comparison applies to the companies, the year and the quantity\n 11578\t      asked, and why, under its own assumptions, that side is more likely; an answer that cannot explain it is not supported;\n 11579\t    - \"先说“还判断不了”\";\n 11580\t    - \"先停在这里\".\n 11581\t- **Alex's answer** (2026-11-23): \"可以发布，要讲清楚 (推荐)\".\n 11582\t- **Decided (Alex).** When the passages do not give the asked quantity, a forecast may rest on a related figure. It is supported\n 11583\t  only when the answer explains two things: why the comparison applies to the companies, the period and the quantity asked; and why,\n 11584\t  under the answer's own stated assumptions, the chosen side of the line is more likely. An answer that cannot explain this is not\n 11585\t  supported.\n 11586\t  - D741 ruling 3 still holds: a total used for a narrower quantity states the deduction as its own figure, or gives a supported\n 11587\t    bound.\n 11588\t  - D745's ruling still holds: a justified unresolved answer counts as usable.\n 11589\t- **Next (D746).** The ruling's words go into the shared reader definitions (`blind_rules.py`). Then one $0 blinded re-read of nine\n 11590\t  stored answers, registered with its expected readings and reviewed by the council before any packet is read.\n   547\t  - Checked on a scratch copy: no recorded premise gives no verdict; with premises it passes; a split invented-cut control fails; an\n   548\t    omitted floor decision waits.\n   549\t  - D749's `TALLY.json` stands as scored under its own registration. Re-running it under the new rules would ask for premise records\n   550\t    it was not registered to need.\n   551\t\n   552\t**[D751] The last $0 writer test of this wording loop (council rounds 67–68; under D439 while Alex is away), registered before any\n   553\twriter is sent.**\n   554\t- **Why.** D750 FAILED on one settled line (`BLIND6-RESULT.md`). E's Claude writer bracketed the pace between the market pace and a\n   555\t  \"more than double\" it treated as a ceiling, and took the midpoint.\n   556\t  - Across four runs that writer forecast $37B, $32.4B, $42B and $35B, each by a new route, none settled supported.\n   557\t  - Council 67's structural reading: an unknown fact decides the side of the line. Is the FY2028 volume in hand? The writers asserted\n   558\t    it one way ($42B) or the other ($35B).\n   559\t  - The writer's own account named the bullet-2 and bullet-3 discount sentences and \"not established … never a reason to stay\".\n   560\t- **The request change** (`judging-v13s.txt`; the D750 text is kept in `judging-v13s-d750.txt`; the registered sha256 `080a0012…` is\n   561\t  checked by `blind7.py build`). In line 9, \"Before sizing a share, deduction, discount or pace that decides the band, explain which\n   562\t  band the evidence favours.\" becomes \"When other evidence bears on the forecast, weigh the plausible alternatives for any unknown\n   563\t  fact that decides the band. Explain whether the evidence as a whole favours one band before deciding whether or how much to adjust.\"\n   564\t  - The size, bound, explained-comparison and unresolved routes are unchanged.\n   565\t  - Size: 4,867 → 4,976 characters, 975 → 993 tokens.\n   566\t  - Checks: cases 111 of 111, rehearsal 19 of 19, the pinned v13 plan unchanged.\n   567\t- **The paper check before the change** (`PAPER-CHECK-D751.md`, verified row by row by council 68):\n   568\t  - it exposes the $42B and $35B premises;\n   569\t  - it preserves both specific \"can't tell\" answers;\n   570\t  - it leaves the $37B answer a boundary case;\n   571\t  - the explained near-cutoff S forecasts stand.\n   572\t  - Council 68 replaced \"across the plausible readings\" with \"weigh the plausible alternatives … whether the evidence as a whole favours\n   573\t    one band\". The first wording could read as requiring a forecast to survive every reading, which is stricter than D747.\n   574\t- **Design and tally.** D750's, unchanged (`blind7.py` wraps `blind5.py`), on a new run folder,\n   575\t  `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind7-2026-11-23/`:\n   576\t  - S, E and P-v8, each with one fresh Claude and one fresh read-only Codex writer;\n   577\t  - the same two controls, each with its original request;\n   578\t  - fresh readers of both families, with adjudication where they disagree;\n   579\t  - the decisive premise and its passage support recorded for every settled S and E vote (council 66).\n   580\t- **Exit rule (council 67):**\n   581\t  - a clean pass goes to Alex for the paid authorization;\n   582\t  - a settled failure or a persistent split ends this wording loop, and the publication choice for E-type cases returns to Alex.\n    34\tSYSTEM = (HERE / 'judging-v13-draft.txt').read_text()\n    35\tSYSTEM_S = (HERE / 'judging-v13s.txt').read_text()                      # the small format (D737): one sentence changed\n    36\tCHECKERS = {None: cc, 'v13': cc, 'v13s': cs}\n    37\tAPPROVED_CEILING = 0.5                                                  # Alex, 2026-11-22 (D732): \"0.5 美元 (推荐)\"\n    38\tPINNED_PLAN_SHA256 = (HERE / 'paid-plan.sha256').read_text().strip() if (HERE / 'paid-plan.sha256').exists() else None\n    39\tAUTH = Path.home() / '.hivemind-keys/v13-paid-gate/authorization.json'\n    40\tDRAWS = (1, 2, 3)\n    41\tRETRY_MARK = '\\n\\nThe code refused the previous answer because: '\n    42\tPAIRS = [('old-1', ('v4', 9014), ('v8', 9022)), ('old-2', ('v14', 8729), ('v9', 8737)), ('old-3', ('v3', 9183), ('v35', 9177))]\n    43\tSINGLETONS = [('O', 'old-2', 'v29', 8734, ('NVIDIA', 'equity investment commitments', 'company', 'end of FY2027')),\n    44\t              ('S', 'old-2', 'v6', 8595, ('four largest AI hyperscalers', 'data-center capex', 'combined', 'CY2027')),\n    45\t              ('E', 'old-3', 'v41', 9166, ('NVIDIA', 'revenue', 'Vera CPU', 'FY2028'))]\n    46\t\n    47\t\n    48\tdef W(population, measure, scope, period):\n    49\t    return {'population': population, 'measure': measure, 'scope': scope, 'period': period}\n    50\t\n463:QUESTIONS = {'usable': 'the outcome is usable and supported by the shown passages it rests on',\n466:             'deciding_gap': 'an unresolved overlap, a change of scope or an unsized adjustment decides its band'}\n763:    usable_pairs = {'joint': {}, 'separate': {}}\n774:                if 'yes' in (r['contradiction'], r['internal_reference'], r['deciding_gap']):\n776:        if k['unit'].startswith('pair') and k['arm'] in usable_pairs:\n777:            usable_pairs[k['arm']].setdefault(k['unit'], []).append(k['decision'] == 'publish' and all(r['usable'] == 'yes' for r in final.values()))\n778:    joint_total = sum(sum(v) for v in usable_pairs['joint'].values())\n779:    separate_total = sum(sum(v) for v in usable_pairs['separate'].values())\n780:    per_snapshot = {u: sum(v) for u, v in usable_pairs['joint'].items()}\n781:    complete = (not problems and len(key) == 27 and len(usable_pairs['joint']) == 3 and len(usable_pairs['separate']) == 3\n782:                and all(len(v) == 3 for arm in usable_pairs.values() for v in arm.values()))\n784:    summary = {'complete': complete, 'problems': problems[:20], 'joint usable pairs': joint_total, 'separate usable pairs': separate_total,\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers\nnl -ba \"$d/SMALL-DESIGN.md\" | sed -n '\"'157,180p;215,236p'\nnl -ba \\\"\"'$d/paid_gate.py\" | sed -n '\"'765,792p'\nnl -ba \\\"\"'$d/PAID-GATE-RESULT.md\" | head -39\nnl -ba \"$d/paid-plan.json\" | head -c 2400\nrg -n '\"'CY2027|CY2026|2027 calendar|calendar 2027|2027.*capex' \\\"\"'$d/paid-plan.json\" -m 2 | cut -c1-450\nls poc/data/judging-v13s-blind7-2026-11-23\nnl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind7-2026-11-23/E/user.txt | head -40 | cut -c1-440\nnl -ba docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md | sed -n '\"'101,\"'$p'\"' | tail -85\ntail -4 docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md | cut -c1-430\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers\nnl -ba \"$d/SMALL-DESIGN.md\" | sed -n '\"'157,180p;215,236p'\nnl -ba \\\"\"'$d/paid_gate.py\" | sed -n '\"'765,792p'\nnl -ba \\\"\"'$d/PAID-GATE-RESULT.md\" | head -39\nnl -ba \"$d/paid-plan.json\" | head -c 2400\nrg -n '\"'CY2027|CY2026|2027 calendar|calendar 2027|2027.*capex' \\\"\"'$d/paid-plan.json\" -m 2 | cut -c1-450\nls poc/data/judging-v13s-blind7-2026-11-23\nnl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind7-2026-11-23/E/user.txt | head -40 | cut -c1-440\nnl -ba docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md | sed -n '\"'101,\"'$p'\"' | tail -85\ntail -4 docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md | cut -c1-430\"","aggregated_output":"   157\t- `merge`'s unification of a shared forecast uses `evaluate`'s values with no bound.\n   158\t- The reader brief is unchanged. Its `deciding_gap` question is where readers judge the recorded conversions and estimates.\n   159\t\n   160\t**State × event:** unchanged from D731. A refusal is retried once, diagnostic only; the first attempt is scored.\n   161\t\n   162\t## 5. The $0 blind test (registered; REPAIR-DESIGN.md §5 with rounds 51's fixes)\n   163\t\n   164\t**Inputs.** Each case's archive, call, question, ladder and unit are registered, and the build refuses a heading that differs.\n   165\t\n   166\t| Case | Archive / call | Key | Ladder edges ($bn) | Role |\n   167\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n   168\t| P (joint), P-v4, P-v8 (separate) | old-1 v4 8810, v8 8816 | NVIDIA revenue company FY2027; NVIDIA revenue Data Center FY2027 | 431.2/392/352.8/313.6; 400/350/300/250 | the pair; 79 quotes absent from the gate |\n   169\t| S | old-3 v6 9043 | four largest AI hyperscalers, data-center capex, combined, CY2026 | 500/600/700/800 | fresh: 41 of 50 quotes absent from the gate |\n   170\t| O | old-2 v29 8507 | NVIDIA equity investment commitments company end of FY2027 (the fiscal year-end balance the annual report publishes) | 25/50/75/100 | control |\n   171\t| E | old-3 v41 9071 | NVIDIA revenue Vera CPU FY2028 | 50/40/30/20 | control |\n   172\t\n   173\t**Part A, code, $0.** Each case asserts its intended rule and message. The schema's validity is checked first, so a schema error\n   174\tcannot mask the rule under test.\n   175\t- **A1, correct forms.** Each passes:\n   176\t  - a $4.5 billion ± 3% guidance as two sources with apply_growth ends;\n   177\t  - Q1 + Q2 = H1;\n   178\t  - FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4;\n   179\t  - a year-over-year rate on last year's quarter (recorded);\n   180\t  - whole × mix through share;\n   215\t**[52] Additions to Part A.**\n   216\t- **A1 adds:**\n   217\t  - the remainder company − Data Center = the rest;\n   218\t  - \"US$10 billion\", \"$96.2bn\", \"roughly twice\", \"between $105.84 billion and $110.16 billion\";\n   219\t  - \"$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\" as a range and as a midpoint;\n   220\t  - a point Data Center forecast beside a company range (S3 holds);\n   221\t  - a final range estimate resting on a computed point of the same quantity (S4 holds).\n   222\t- **A2 adds:**\n   223\t  - Data Center × 1x added to company (S8);\n   224\t  - an estimate resting on both part and whole (recorded, not refused, and the record asserted);\n   225\t  - two Data Center Q4 figures on two paths (S4);\n   226\t  - Q3 guidance used directly beside a Q3 forecast (S4);\n   227\t  - an estimate itself marked as the reference (S5);\n   228\t  - a computed reference with basis forecast (S5);\n   229\t  - a declared value within tolerance whose recomputed value crosses the rung edge (S6 on the recomputed value);\n   230\t  - a part whose recomputed lowest value exceeds the whole's recomputed highest, while its declared value does not (S3);\n   231\t  - sum($108B, 2%) (S2 unit);\n   232\t  - division by [-1, 1] (S2);\n   233\t  - [-3, -2] × [2, 4] declared other than [-12, -4] (S2);\n   234\t  - a source as the outcome (S6);\n   235\t  - a figure id equal to a passage id (S7);\n   236\t  - \"plus or minus 2%\" copied as \"2%\" and used as +2% for the range (S1);\n   765\t    for pid, k in key.items():\n   766\t        if pid not in readings:\n   767\t            continue\n   768\t        final, probs = final_readings(pid, readings[pid], list(k['cards']), k['grounds'])\n   769\t        problems += probs\n   770\t        if final is None:\n   771\t            continue\n   772\t        if k['decision'] == 'publish' and k['arm'] in ('joint', 'single'):      # D725: defects across the joint and singleton answers\n   773\t            for v, r in final.items():\n   774\t                if 'yes' in (r['contradiction'], r['internal_reference'], r['deciding_gap']):\n   775\t                    defects.append((pid, k['unit'], k['arm'], k['cards'][v]))\n   776\t        if k['unit'].startswith('pair') and k['arm'] in usable_pairs:\n   777\t            usable_pairs[k['arm']].setdefault(k['unit'], []).append(k['decision'] == 'publish' and all(r['usable'] == 'yes' for r in final.values()))\n   778\t    joint_total = sum(sum(v) for v in usable_pairs['joint'].values())\n   779\t    separate_total = sum(sum(v) for v in usable_pairs['separate'].values())\n   780\t    per_snapshot = {u: sum(v) for u, v in usable_pairs['joint'].items()}\n   781\t    complete = (not problems and len(key) == 27 and len(usable_pairs['joint']) == 3 and len(usable_pairs['separate']) == 3\n   782\t                and all(len(v) == 3 for arm in usable_pairs.values() for v in arm.values()))\n   783\t    passes = complete and not defects and joint_total >= 8 and all(n >= 2 for n in per_snapshot.values()) and joint_total - separate_total >= 3\n   784\t    summary = {'complete': complete, 'problems': problems[:20], 'joint usable pairs': joint_total, 'separate usable pairs': separate_total,\n   785\t               'joint per snapshot': per_snapshot, 'published defects': defects,\n   786\t               'verdict': 'PASS' if passes else ('INCOMPLETE' if not complete else 'STOP')}\n   787\t    print(json.dumps(summary, indent=1))\n   788\t    return summary\n   789\t\n   790\t\n   791\tif __name__ == '__main__':\n   792\t    cmd = sys.argv[1] if len(sys.argv) > 1 else ''\n     1\t# judging-v13 paid gate: result (D733)\n     2\t\n     3\t**STOP.** Every one of the 72 answers was refused by the code checks: 36 scored first attempts and 36 diagnostic retries. Nothing\n     4\twas published, and the joint arm is usable in 0 of 9 pairs against the registered 8 of 9.\n     5\t\n     6\tThe result does not answer the question the gate was built for: does judging related number questions in one call give better\n     7\tnumbers than judging each alone? It shows that the answer format, as the request stated it, could not be filled. Most refusals are\n     8\trules the request never stated, or checks that refuse right answers. A few are the model's own errors.\n     9\t\n    10\t- **Run:** `poc/data/judging-v13-paid-gate-2026-11-22/`, sent once (D732) with gpt-6-luna. The ledger records 72 attempts and\n    11\t  $0.3677 of the $0.50 ceiling: $0.1554 for the first attempts and $0.2124 for the retries. Every attempt finished with status\n    12\t  `answered`, so the result is complete, not INCOMPLETE.\n    13\t- **The blind reading** was not run. The registered brief says \"A card that has no answer cannot be usable\", and every card has\n    14\t  none, so the readers cannot change the verdict.\n    15\t- **Council round 48:**\n    16\t  - `round48-brief.md`;\n    17\t  - `claude-r48.md`, Claude's reflection, written first;\n    18\t  - `codex-r48.md`, an independent fresh read-only Codex session that reproduced every refusal from the journals and the requests.\n    19\t\n    20\t## 1. Census (first attempt → retry; Codex's count, which corrects Claude's)\n    21\t\n    22\t| Attempt | Arm | Answers | Structure (C7) messages / answers | Stopped at the structure check |\n    23\t|---|---|---:|---:|---:|\n    24\t| First | Joint | 9 | 39 / 9 | 9 |\n    25\t| First | Separate | 18 | 47 / 18 | 17 |\n    26\t| First | Single | 9 | 13 / 9 | 8 |\n    27\t| Retry | Joint | 9 | 40 / 9 | 9 |\n    28\t| Retry | Separate | 18 | 41 / 18 | 14 |\n    29\t| Retry | Single | 9 | 12 / 9 | 6 |\n    30\t\n    31\t`check()` returns early after any structure error, so most source, arithmetic and compatibility checks never ran on these answers.\n    32\t\n    33\t**Prose where the check expects an id**, counted by occurrence:\n    34\t\n    35\t| Field | First | Retry |\n    36\t|---|---:|---:|\n    37\t| `answers[].outcome` | 45 of 45 | 45 of 45 |\n    38\t| `answers[].reference.figure` | 39 | 32 |\n    39\t| `figures[].evidence` (139 of them on source figures, where C1 needs a passage id) | 175 | 133 (95) |\n     1\t[\n     2\t {\n     3\t  \"unit\": \"pair-old-1\",\n     4\t  \"arm\": \"joint\",\n     5\t  \"draw_store\": \"old-1\",\n     6\t  \"members\": [\n     7\t   \"v4\",\n     8\t   \"v8\"\n     9\t  ],\n    10\t  \"system\": \"You are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging the current rungs of a family of related number theses: one company's quantities that overlap, such as a total and its segment, a level and its growth, or a later year that rests on an earlier year's base. Use only the supplied evidence and themes in force. Each rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\\n\\n- Judge every member from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently) and the themes in force, as if for the first time. Earlier forecasts are not shown: whether a conclusion changed is compared afterwards.\\n- Judge each member against a reference: what the market expects for that question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on its ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate that carries the guided growth forward, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. Build the family's figures once and read every member's outcome from them: a part cannot exceed its whole, a figure that spans several periods or products gives a question only its share, and a quantity the family shares has one forecast per scenario. Compare each outcome with its rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\\n- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \\\"not established\\\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and pri11:  \"user\": \"[Family] members:\\nThesis v4 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"NVIDIA total revenue in FY2027\\\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What will NVIDIA's total revenue be in FY2027?\\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: Total revenue is at least $431.2 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: Total revenue is at least $392 billion and below $431.2 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Total revenue is at least $352.8 billion and below $392 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: Total revenue is at\n1239:    \"text\": \"Micron's most recent guidance was that tight conditions will \\\"persist beyond calendar 2027.\\\"\",\nCHECKED.json\nE\nP-v8\nS\nTALLY.json\nadjudication\ncodex-writers.log\nkey-print.txt\nreader-key.json\nreader-packets\nreasons.json\n     1\t[Family] members:\n     2\tThesis v41 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA Vera CPU revenue\", FY2028, due 2028-02-29): What will NVIDIA report as Vera CPU revenue for FY2028?\n     3\tLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $50 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $40 billion and less than $50 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $30 billion and less than $40 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $20 billion and less than $30 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is less than $20 bill\n     4\t\n     5\tRelations: none.\n     6\tShared quantities, one forecast figure each; on every arithmetic path from an outcome, through rates and multiples too, the first figure of that quantity reached is its forecast: none.\n     7\tOutcome keys (population | measure | scope | period), in USD bn, to be used exactly: v41 = NVIDIA | revenue | Vera CPU | FY2028.\n     8\t\n     9\t[Evidence in view for the family, 15 items]\n    10\t- e3660 [party_statement; Bram Berkowitz, fool.com, reporting Colette Kress's statement; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:fd25a8ea6f60290264bfbe55 2026-08-30] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA still expects $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 and expects FY2028 CPU revenue to more than double that amount.\n    11\t    Quote: The bad news for AMD and Intel is that Kress said Nvidia not only still expects $20 billion in CPU revenue in its fiscal 2027, but expects that number to more than double in fiscal 2028.\n    12\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3302, e4691, e4856; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    13\t- e4687 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source AMD ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] AMD estimates that server CPU market revenue could grow at a 50% annual rate through 2030, reaching a $220 billion opportunity by the end of the decade.\n    14\t    Quote: AMD notes that the server CPU market's revenue could increase at an annual rate of 50% through 2030, creating a $220 billion revenue opportunity by the end of the decade.\n    15\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4084; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    16\t- e4689 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst), quoting Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said Vera CPU deployments would include every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\n    17\t    Quote: Kress added that the Vera CPU will be deployed by \"every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\"\n    18\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2769; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    19\t- e4691 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] NVIDIA expects Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 to be double its FY2027 level.\n    20\t    Quote: What's more, Nvidia expects its Vera CPU revenue to double next year.\n    21\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3660, e4764; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    22\t- e4692 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst), citing Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] Tom's Hardware reported that Arm-based servers represented 45% of data-center market revenue share in June.\n    23\t    Quote: Tom's Hardware noted in June that Arm-based servers account for 45% of the data center market's revenue share.\n    24\t- e4764 [party_statement; fool.com (Keith Noonan); original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:0ba93692dc5df4b999fe3729 2026-09-11] Jensen Huang forecast that NVIDIA CPU revenue would more than double in FY2028.\n    25\t    Quote: Huang expects CPU revenue to more than double next fiscal year\n    26\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4691; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    27\t- e4856 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:c70bc12967b9b53426b5fb10 2026-09-12] NVIDIA management said Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 could be more than double the $20 billion potential it cited for FY2027.\n    28\t    Quote: with the potential to more than double that number in fiscal year 2028.\n    29\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3660; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    30\t- e4857 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Bank of America analysts ; from gnews:c70bc12967b9b53426b5fb10 2026-09-12] Bank of America analysts forecast that the CPU market would grow fivefold, from $35 billion in 2026 to $170 billion in 2030.\n    31\t    Quote: Analysts at Bank of America also released a research report in June, saying they believe the CPU market will grow fivefold between this year and 2030, from $35 billion to $170 billion.\n    32\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e935; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    33\t- e5044 [party_statement; Insider Monkey; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a1af1bb8840fa4ec9e52bcb0 2026-09-15] NVIDIA said Anthropic is evaluating NVIDIA’s Vera CPU for CPU-intensive agentic workloads.\n    34\t    Quote: Nvidia recently said Anthropic is evaluating its Vera CPU for CPU-intensive agentic workloads,\n    35\t- e5399 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPUs delivered 20% higher per-core performance than NVIDIA’s 88-core Vera CPU in SPEC 2026.\n    36\t    Quote: Here, the AMD 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPUs provide a 20% uplift over NVIDIA's 88-Core Vera CPU.\n    37\t- e5400 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its EPYC 9996 delivered 1.2 times Vera-based platform per-core performance and 2.24 times its platform-level performance in SPECrate 2026 Integer testing.\n    38\t    Quote: In SPECrate 2026 Integer testing, the AMD EPYC 9996 server CPU delivers 1.2 times the per-core performance of an Nvidia Vera-based platform and 2.24 times its platform-level performance.\n    39\t- e5401 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD estimated that an EPYC 9996 system in a modeled 100-kilowatt rack would deliver 3.4 times the throughput of a Vera-based platform.\n    40\t    Quote: In a modeled 100-kilowatt rack, it delivers an estimated 3.4 times the throughput of a Vera-based platform.\n   117\t4. Change only the one place that holds the filing rule; report its length in characters before and after, and write down the reason for any growth\n   118\t   (this time's reason: examples replace one abstract sentence).\n   119\t\n   120\t## 6. How to measure (the ruler does not move; the method does not change again)\n   121\t\n   122\t- **Requests**: rebuild each stored request byte for byte with the production code, changing only the one place that holds the filing rule; the\n   123\t  rebuilt request's archive id must equal the original's, or it is not sent (`resend_filing.py`, AGENTS §5). Never re-send the archive's copy whose\n   124\t  keys were sorted alphabetically.\n   125\t- **Articles**: development set = the 54 articles of `selection.json` (already read many times); held-out set = articles the extraction gold read and\n   126\t  the judgement gold uses that are not among those 54, drawn once before the test, with the list frozen and never changed afterwards. Both sets are\n   127\t  measured and reported apart.\n   128\t- **Control**: the unchanged requests re-sent 3 times the same day. No comparison with the archive (model drift).\n   129\t- **Each answer**: goes through the production code checks and filing first (including \"keep only the narrowest\" and \"no home means dropped\"), and is\n   130\t  counted after.\n   131\t- **What is counted** (`measure_filing.py`, with its matching rule fixed: items are matched on the distinct numbers and the proper nouns other than\n   132\t  NVIDIA in the source text; a source passage the system quotes that overlaps the gold's passage also counts as the same matter; \"optional\" counts as\n   133\t  optional; the citations in the body of the gold's answers are read):\n   134\t  - whether each fact that matters (one the gold uses to judge any question) reached the question that uses it, counted per fact, not per \"fact ×\n   135\t    question\";\n   136\t  - per question: the facts that matter that reached it and the new noise that appeared, counted by the decision rule below;\n   137\t  - the parking lot: how many facts the total-revenue question (v1, together with v22) takes in, and what they are;\n   138\t  - noise: how many of the sentences the standard does not keep are kept; per question, how noise is filed (links on facts the standard does not keep\n   139\t    or no question uses);\n   140\t  - how many core and supporting facts appear; the facts that matter that are dropped; how many questions each fact is filed under on average.\n   141\t  - Matching still has a small error (spot check: the new rule mismatches a few and misses a few); say so when reporting the numbers, and read the\n   142\t    ones that matter one by one.\n   143\t- **Decision rule** (rewritten on 2026-11-04 with Alex's agreement, D360. The original draft required \"every draw above the highest of the control's\n   144\t  three\" and the like, which the control itself fails when measured again, so it cannot tell good from bad. Script `decide.py`; written down and frozen\n   145\t  before each test; each draw's numbers and the totals are all reported):\n   146\t  - **Totals, with luck ruled out first**: each article's 6 answers from the new and the old arm are randomly reassigned between the two arms 4,000\n   147\t    times (with a fixed random seed); the new rule's difference counts as a real change only when fewer than 5% of the reassignments reach it.\n   148\t    - Development set: facts that matter reaching the question that uses them must rise; the count under the total-revenue questions (v1, v22) must\n   149\t      fall; sentences the standard does not keep must not rise; core facts must not fall.\n   150\t    - Held-out set: facts that matter reaching the question that uses them must not fall; sentences the standard does not keep must not rise; core\n   151\t      facts must not fall.\n   152\t  - **Per question, counted by code against the gold** (development and held-out sets together):\n   153\t    - A fact that matters which reaches the question at least two fewer times under the new rule than under the control counts as \"one fewer\" for that\n   154\t      question; in under the control all three times and under the new rule none of the three, it counts as \"lost outright\". A move to another question\n   155\t      that the gold also uses it for and that is no broader than the original (one home per fact, D207) does not count as fewer; a move to a broader\n   156\t      question counts. \"One more\" in the opposite direction offsets it.\n   157\t    - A noise sentence (not kept by the standard, or kept but used by no question) that the new rule files under the question at least twice while the\n   158\t      control never does counts as \"new noise\"; filed all three times, it counts as \"outright new noise\". The opposite direction offsets it.\n   159\t    - Any question with a net 3 fewer, a net 2 lost outright, a net 5 more new noise, or a net 3 more outright new noise fails. These thresholds are the\n   160\t      smallest values that keep \"misjudged when new and old are exactly the same\" within about one in ten; at the test, first compute the\n   161\t      misjudgement rate from the reassignments; if it is above one in ten, raise the threshold most prone to misjudgement by one step, then compare with\n   162\t      what was measured.\n   163\t  - **Smaller changes** (a question 2 fewer, two or three more noise) cannot be told from luck in three measurements: all are listed, given to a reader\n   164\t    who did not write the rule to read against the raw answers, and written into the report; they do not decide pass or fail.\n   165\t  Only when everything passes does it become a candidate; a candidate runs end to end three times on the 14-day window (extraction re-sent, matching and\n   166\t  judging run as usual, scored on the frozen judgement ruler), and that step needs Alex's word.\n   167\t\n   168\t## 7. Process (no drift)\n   169\t\n   170\t- **One recommendation and one test at a time** (AGENTS §7.3, Alex: \"就测试一种，一次\"): write the design first and put it through rounds of review (own\n   171\t  checks, Codex read-only, a reader who did not write it reading the raw records) until a round finds nothing serious; write down and freeze the\n   172\t  predictions and stop conditions; test once; if it does not match, stop and take it to Alex (D355: \"如果有什么不符合hypothesis 的地方你需要终止然后和我说我们重新评估\").\n   173\t- **A review's report is a lead**: each finding counts only once it is checked against the records (AGENTS §1).\n   174\t- **A prompt audit before any prompt change** (`.claude/skills/prompt-audit/SKILL.md`): check the whole request against the standard sentence by\n   175\t  sentence, in both directions.\n   176\t- **Every step goes into `log.md`**: what was done, what came of it, the files; mistakes and overturned results stay in it too.\n   177\t- **Do not repeat what was ruled out** (§3).\n   178\t\n   179\t## 8. Steps and where we are\n   180\t\n   181\t| # | Step | Output | Status |\n   182\t|---|---|---|---|\n   183\t| 1 | Design: the classes of indirect relation (a closed set + examples from other industries), how to file when a fact measures both sides, deleting the fallback; checked against the standard sentence by sentence; length before and after | `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/design-links.md` | Done (draft 3, v74l / poc-v115) |\n   184\t| 2 | Checks at no cost: a sample answer written by the new rule goes through every code check; the new rule's text goes through the prompt audit | Same as above | Done |\n   185\t| 3 | Review rounds (Codex, independent readers, own), until nothing serious remains | `review-links-*.md` | Done (Codex 7 rounds, independent readers 3 times; round 7 found nothing serious) |\n   186\t| 4 | Draw and freeze the held-out set; write down and freeze this test's predictions and decision rule | `heldout.json`, `predictions-links.md` | Done (sha256 recorded for both) |\n   187\t| 5 | Test once: development set + held-out set, the new rule and the same-day control 3 times each | `answers-links.json`, `log.md` | Done (2026-11-04): failed: facts that matter reaching the right question, development set +7 (p 0.26); every other condition passed |\n   188\t| 6 | If passed: to Alex, to decide whether to run three 14-day end-to-end draws; if failed: stop, to Alex | `design-links.md` §8 | Stopped, to Alex |\n   189\t| 7 | (D361, Alex: \"按照你的提以来，但是你一定是自己反思多轮之后才给出的行动\") Measure the ceiling first: hand the source passages the gold cites straight to the frozen judgement; the custom-chip, operating-margin and capital-spending questions judged 3 times each at the window end, the custom-chip question as it was 3 times as the control; the fallback sentence is not deleted for now | `reflection-v74l.md`, `oracle-design.md` | Done: operating margin grounded 3/3, capital spending 2/3, custom chips 0/3 (the gap is in interpretation); the next step waits for Alex |\n   190\t| 8 | (D362, Alex: \"你继续啊不要scope drift and strongly follow evidence of your discovery and learning\") Two small changes where facts are not delivered, one at a time: ① when a quote is rewritten, code matches it back to the source sentence (changing nothing the model sees; verified by replaying the archive); ② a figure question's description states which figures it is derived from by definition (first sent 3 times each on the article that motivated it) | `delivery-fixes.md` | Stopped: an independent check overturned the first change's code (it would take in wrong figures); the second failed its trial send (1/3). The root: the signed standard's §5.3 does not keep two key facts, treating them as \"a restatement of known facts\" (proposal 6, awaiting approval); to Alex |\n   191\t| 9 | **Historical; superseded by D370.** (D363, Alex: \"乙\") At coverage start, record each question's starting figure once from the company's latest earnings report; the daily path still takes no restatements: design, rounds of self-checks and independent checks, validated first on the archive in the simplest way | `baseline-design.md` | Validated on two questions: the frozen extraction does not read the report's tables (0/3); with each question reading the report once on its own, the operating-margin question read its starting figure 2/3 and the capital-spending question 3/3, and the judgement then judged right (2/3, 3/3), about 2.5 cents. All 22 questions tested once (2026-11-05, Alex: \"你充分验证\"; files chosen by code, a same-day control, about $0.43): v10 and v12 judged right 9/9 each; no question already judged right turned wrong; but 5 of the 53 figures read were other quantities (the Vera CPU question and the Blackwell and Rubin question took a larger or neighbouring revenue line), so it failed condition 3, written beforehand. The model itself pointed to the reading instructions' sentence \"write the lines it is computed from, or the line the report uses for it\"; stopped, to Alex. Alex: \"改\"; only this sentence was changed (\"write only the lines it is computed from, or the same quantity under another name; never a broader line or a line next to it\"), tried on 4 questions first, then tested on all 22 (about $0.36): all three conditions passed: v10 and v12 9/9 each, no question judged right turned wrong, and all 46 figures were the questions' own quantities. Checked after an independent reader's challenge: the new sentence made the reading miss the capital-spending line in 3 of 12 reads, and it had been tested only on the one May report; the fourth version (a line-by-line re-check after writing) removed too much and failed; after a census of every failure trace (Alex: \"我需要你深度看完所有失败trace给我准信\"), the third version with the repaired quote check was chosen (D365): 0 wrong figures on the held-out August report; built into the production system (poc-v116; the $0 checks and the tests all pass). The full acceptance, three 14-day draws (Alex: \"跑验收\"; a same-day control; about $4.79): all passed: operating margin and capital spending judged right 3/3 each (the control never judged them), 53 against 45 in total (of 57); for the China-share question, the only one two fewer, both readers found the starting figures did not lead it astray. Alex: \"都做\": poc-v116 became the default (D366); the code was committed and a PR opened |\n   192\t\n   193\t## 9. Alex's own words on this matter (in time order)\n   194\t\n   195\t- \"你这个单独开一个专题出来解决 … 是promot，是系统结构设计还是到底哪里有问题挂错一堆东西，机制是不是有问题（一定要挂吗？有没有可能是杂音\" (2026-11-03)\n   196\t- \"So if it's a customer problem or other companies that you think is relating NVIDIA and it's the wrong evidence, then what is the standard for it? … from a hedge fund analyst standpoint, are these even relevant? Do you think your ruler is wrong in this aspect?\" (2026-11-04)\n   197\t- \"你确定下你接下来要改进的量化目标，以及你要看到端到端我们的管线是要做什么的，不要乱硬优化，要看到目标\" (2026-11-04)\n   198\t- \"就测试一种，一次 … 能不能自己有个超级自信的定论和证据再给我提建议\" (2026-11-04)\n   199\t- \"这些只是类型题，你需要抽象拔高我们现在的核心问题和卡点。这个很重要这样改下去才是对的\" (2026-11-04)\n   200\t- \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\" (2026-11-04)\n   201\t- \"go\" (2026-11-04, on the proposed amendments to §5.2 and §6 and on this test)\n| 38 | 11-05 | Alex：\"好，按这三步做，你不要再搬石头砸自己脚了，你要收敛上生产\"。第 1 步：引用检查搬进正式代码 `src/hivemind/baseline.py`，加\"行名是报告的、数在同一行就存原文那一整行\"的修复（漏脚注记号、跳格）；对照 36 例全对，三个弄坏的版本各抓出 1 个错；重放全部存下的读法，改变的正好是那 6 个被拒的对数。第 2 步：第三版加修好的检查读 8 月留出财报 132 次（0.096 美元），两人分类 | 第 1 步：只改了那 6 条。第 2 步过：44 个数 0 个错（3 条\"剩余资本开支承诺 70 亿\"两人不一致，对着原文定为\"自己的量，别的期间\"）；v10 3/3、v12 2/3 | 读法定为第三版加修好的检查，不再改；第 3 步写正式代码 |\n| 39 | 11-05 | 第 3 步：做进正式系统（设计四张表，Codex 审 3 轮：第 1 轮 4 条严重、第 2 轮 2 条严重，都改；第 3 轮无严重问题） | 建成 `baseline.run` + poc-v116 + baseline-v3 提示词文件；$0 核对：44 份请求和验证时逐字节相同，3 次存下的回答走正式代码写出的记录完全一样，第一天判断正好判这些题，网页从开始跟踪那天可见；新测试 12 条、6 个弄坏版本都抓出、全套 272 条通过；设计页和 PRD 00/03/04/05/06 更新，`docs/check.py` 通过 | 交 Alex：要不要跑 14 天三遍完整验收（约 5 美元） |\n| 40 | 11-05 | Alex：\"跑验收\"。14 天三遍完整验收：poc-v116（开始跟踪时读财报）三遍、当天重跑 poc-v108 三遍作对照，冻结尺子打分（事先锁定 `acceptance-design.md`）；电脑中途在电池上睡了约 5 小时，6 遍被暂停，插电后自己接着跑完 | **全过**：v10、v12 各 3/3（对照从没判过）；合计 53 对 45（满分 57，+8）；唯一少两次的 v17（0/3 对 2/3）两人都认定不是起点数：同一份抽取请求，新配置两遍碰巧留下\"中国份额归零\"那句，对照没留；约 4.79 美元 | 交 Alex：poc-v116 是否成为默认 |\n| 41 | 11-05 | Alex：\"都做\"（poc-v116 设成默认；提交代码、开 PR）。默认配置改成 poc-v116，默认的条目数据库换成带财报网页原文的那一份（旧的没有原文，新默认会在它上面拒跑）；测试按用户的用法补上：不给种子题就拒绝，给了就先读财报再跑第一天 | 测试全过，5 个弄坏的版本都抓出；配置的设置和验收三遍记下的校验码一致（只改了配置里的注释）；花钱前的检查脚本在新配置的运行数据上照常跑完，但不复查读财报那一步的引用检查（记为未补的缺口）；D366 | 提交、开 PR；下一类问题等 Alex |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=poc/data/judging-v13s-blind7-2026-11-23\nnl -ba \"$d/E/user.txt\" | head -38 | cut -c1-370\nrg -n '\"'70%|85%|more than double|orders' \\\"\"'$d/E/answers/claude.json\" \"$d/E/answers/codex.json\" | cut -c1-350 | head -22\nnl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r67.md | cut -c1-310 | head -15\nrg -n '\"'v13s|first attempt' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md | head -12\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=poc/data/judging-v13s-blind7-2026-11-23\nnl -ba \"$d/E/user.txt\" | head -38 | cut -c1-370\nrg -n '\"'70%|85%|more than double|orders' \\\"\"'$d/E/answers/claude.json\" \"$d/E/answers/codex.json\" | cut -c1-350 | head -22\nnl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r67.md | cut -c1-310 | head -15\nrg -n '\"'v13s|first attempt' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md | head -12\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t[Family] members:\n     2\tThesis v41 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA Vera CPU revenue\", FY2028, due 2028-02-29): What will NVIDIA report as Vera CPU revenue for FY2028?\n     3\tLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $50 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $40 billion and less than $50 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $30 billion and less than $40 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $20 billion and less than $30 billion. / \n     4\t\n     5\tRelations: none.\n     6\tShared quantities, one forecast figure each; on every arithmetic path from an outcome, through rates and multiples too, the first figure of that quantity reached is its forecast: none.\n     7\tOutcome keys (population | measure | scope | period), in USD bn, to be used exactly: v41 = NVIDIA | revenue | Vera CPU | FY2028.\n     8\t\n     9\t[Evidence in view for the family, 15 items]\n    10\t- e3660 [party_statement; Bram Berkowitz, fool.com, reporting Colette Kress's statement; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:fd25a8ea6f60290264bfbe55 2026-08-30] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA still expects $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 and expects FY2028 CPU revenue to more than double that amount.\n    11\t    Quote: The bad news for AMD and Intel is that Kress said Nvidia not only still expects $20 billion in CPU revenue in its fiscal 2027, but expects that number to more than double in fiscal 2028.\n    12\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3302, e4691, e4856; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    13\t- e4687 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source AMD ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] AMD estimates that server CPU market revenue could grow at a 50% annual rate through 2030, reaching a $220 billion opportunity by the end of the decade.\n    14\t    Quote: AMD notes that the server CPU market's revenue could increase at an annual rate of 50% through 2030, creating a $220 billion revenue opportunity by the end of the decade.\n    15\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4084; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    16\t- e4689 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst), quoting Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said Vera CPU deployments would include every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\n    17\t    Quote: Kress added that the Vera CPU will be deployed by \"every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\"\n    18\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2769; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    19\t- e4691 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] NVIDIA expects Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 to be double its FY2027 level.\n    20\t    Quote: What's more, Nvidia expects its Vera CPU revenue to double next year.\n    21\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3660, e4764; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    22\t- e4692 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst), citing Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] Tom's Hardware reported that Arm-based servers represented 45% of data-center market revenue share in June.\n    23\t    Quote: Tom's Hardware noted in June that Arm-based servers account for 45% of the data center market's revenue share.\n    24\t- e4764 [party_statement; fool.com (Keith Noonan); original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:0ba93692dc5df4b999fe3729 2026-09-11] Jensen Huang forecast that NVIDIA CPU revenue would more than double in FY2028.\n    25\t    Quote: Huang expects CPU revenue to more than double next fiscal year\n    26\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4691; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    27\t- e4856 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:c70bc12967b9b53426b5fb10 2026-09-12] NVIDIA management said Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 could be more than double the $20 billion potential it cited for FY2027.\n    28\t    Quote: with the potential to more than double that number in fiscal year 2028.\n    29\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3660; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    30\t- e4857 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Bank of America analysts ; from gnews:c70bc12967b9b53426b5fb10 2026-09-12] Bank of America analysts forecast that the CPU market would grow fivefold, from $35 billion in 2026 to $170 billion in 2030.\n    31\t    Quote: Analysts at Bank of America also released a research report in June, saying they believe the CPU market will grow fivefold between this year and 2030, from $35 billion to $170 billion.\n    32\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e935; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    33\t- e5044 [party_statement; Insider Monkey; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a1af1bb8840fa4ec9e52bcb0 2026-09-15] NVIDIA said Anthropic is evaluating NVIDIA’s Vera CPU for CPU-intensive agentic workloads.\n    34\t    Quote: Nvidia recently said Anthropic is evaluating its Vera CPU for CPU-intensive agentic workloads,\n    35\t- e5399 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPUs delivered 20% higher per-core performance than NVIDIA’s 88-core Vera CPU in SPEC 2026.\n    36\t    Quote: Here, the AMD 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPUs provide a 20% uplift over NVIDIA's 88-Core Vera CPU.\n    37\t- e5400 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its EPYC 9996 delivered 1.2 times Vera-based platform per-core performance and 2.24 times its platform-level performance in SPECrate 2026 Integer testing.\n    38\t    Quote: In SPECrate 2026 Integer testing, the AMD EPYC 9996 server CPU delivers 1.2 times the per-core performance of an Nvidia Vera-based platform and 2.24 times its platform-level performance.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind7-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json:188:      \"basis_text\": \"My forecast of FY2028 Vera CPU revenue growth. NVIDIA guides to more than double (F2, a floor of 100% growth), but this is a full-year target for the next fiscal year for a product whose deployments are still ahead, and no order, backlog, unit or price figure put\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind7-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json:219:      \"reasons\": \"Reference (rung 2): NVIDIA's own guidance. CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA still expects $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 (F1) and expects FY2028 to more than double it (F2) (e3660); e4691 and e4856 report the same claim as Vera CPU revenue, so the $20 billion is \n     1\t1. **Serious — E repeats one mistake.** I recommend **(b), one change to how the writer chooses a forecast, followed by one $0 test.** On the same 15 passages, the D749 Claude writer said Vera was already selling at volume and forecast $42B. The D750 writer read future deployments as grounds for a disc\n     2\t\n     3\t2. **Serious — the two instructions pull in different directions, but neither licenses the $35B answer.** Guidance governs when it stands alone. The discount applies to a product that has yet to ship. E has other evidence, and that evidence leaves shipment status open. The writer nevertheless used mark\n     4\t\n     5\t3. **Serious — the paid run can still decide whether calculating related answers together helps.** It scores 36 first attempts as 9 joint pairs, 9 reconciled separate pairs and 9 singletons. There are exactly two ways to clear the pair thresholds: **8 usable joint pairs with at most 5 usable separate p\n     6\t\n     7\t4. **Minor for the next decision — one S objection is a real labeling defect.** I would reject the 104% mark. The format calls for both extremes of a ratio, and $130B divided by $125–145B gives 89.7–104%. The answer uses about 96% at the midpoint and never forecasts Meta spending 104% of its total. I w\n     8\t\n     9\t5. **Wording — qualify the claim that S converged.** D750’s two fresh S forecasts earned support, with the two defect marks above still split. D749’s corrected X08 **support** verdict was itself split: one reader accepted $710.4B while treating $730B as a floor. “All four supported” overstates the corr\n    10\t\n    11\t6. **One recommendation — put the choice before the calculation.** Keep the existing guidance-alone rule. Replace the opening sentence of the final evidence check with this wording: **“When other evidence bears on the forecast, say which band it favours before choosing any adjustment. If that choice de\n    12\t\n    13\t7. **One test — first check the distinction on paper, then obtain one fresh blind result.** Before sending anything, apply the proposed words to all eight stored E replies. They must expose the unsupported premises in both $42B and $35B, preserve the two specific “can’t tell” answers, and explain the e\n1:# judging-v13 small format (v13s): code refuses only the five defects and broken structure (D737)\n21:| Today's rule (DESIGN.md) | v13s |\n147:  `render_v13.v13s_schema` builds the schema; `judging-v13s.txt` holds the system text.\n148:- **The plan.** `paid_gate.item` takes a format. A v13s plan (`paid-plan-v13s.json`, pinned) uses `calc_small`. A plan without a\n150:- **Rehearsal and mutants.** The rehearsal takes the plan path. Its stub answers are written in v13s. Each rule S1–S8 gets a mutant.\n160:**State × event:** unchanged from D731. A refusal is retried once, diagnostic only; the first attempt is scored.\n204:- **A3, Luna's observed mistakes** from both journals, each encoded in v13s. Each is impossible under the schema or refused with a\n240:- **A4, the end to end rehearsal.** One v13s reply goes through the first attempt, the separate-arm reconciliation, the card render and\n276:**A clean result** goes to Alex as the request for the paid re-send: the same 36 requests rebuilt in v13s, under a new decision with\n309:- **What is re-read.** The same 12 first replies of `judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22`. Writers are not re-run, and the request is\n352:- **Request changes** (`judging-v13s.txt`; the v13 request is untouched, its plan hash is unchanged):\n363:- **Scope** (`blind4.py`; run folder `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/`):\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '5p' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r67.md\nnl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md | sed -n '119,135p'\nnl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md | sed -n '473,497p'\nrg -n '85%|70%|unknown|order book' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind7-2026-11-23/E/answers/{claude,codex}.json | cut -c1-450 | head -14\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '5p' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r67.md\nnl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md | sed -n '119,135p'\nnl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md | sed -n '473,497p'\nrg -n '85%|70%|unknown|order book' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind7-2026-11-23/E/answers/{claude,codex}.json | cut -c1-450 | head -14\"","aggregated_output":"3. **Serious — the paid run can still decide whether calculating related answers together helps.** It scores 36 first attempts as 9 joint pairs, 9 reconciled separate pairs and 9 singletons. There are exactly two ways to clear the pair thresholds: **8 usable joint pairs with at most 5 usable separate pairs, or 9 with at most 6**. Each snapshot must supply at least 2 of 3 usable joint pairs. If the separate calculation gets 7, even a perfect joint result cannot pass. A supported P-v8 answer alone says nothing about that gain. O, S and E contribute zero pairs to the gain, but one published contradiction, internal reference or gap that decides the band on any joint or singleton answer stops a complete run. A singleton’s unsupported vote alone does not stop it. An unresolved singleton is published and inspected too. The proxy results establish neither Luna’s stop rate nor an inevitable stop. A pass earns production **design**. Alex explicitly kept the paid re-send on hold pending a clean $0 pass. D749 was inconclusive and D750 failed, so option (a) would require him to waive that condition. Option (d) would abandon a comparison that can still decide something. [Paid plan:9](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:9) · [Verdict code:772](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:772) · [Singleton rule:69](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND4-RESULT.md:69) · [Meaning of a pass:278](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:278) · [Alex’s condition:11651](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11651)\n   119\t     - S's Claude reply is split at both levels. Its 95–100% share sets the range, and Codex says the passages do not support 95% as\n   120\t       the low end.\n   121\t     - Both writers met D741 ruling 3's letter (a stated deduction), but not its \"supported\" part. That is a judgement weakness of the\n   122\t       writers on this question, caught by the readers, not an instrument error.\n   123\t- **Other settled support:**\n   124\t  - E's Codex reply is now \"not supported\" by both readers. Its 15% cut from the guided $40B floor decides rung 3 without passage\n   125\t    support. Earlier readers had supported it, and the two-step brief (name the evidence for the band-setting assumption) made readers\n   126\t    stricter on such cuts.\n   127\t  - O's Codex reply is supported after adjudication; O's Claude reply is split again.\n   128\t- **Usable replies per case:**\n   129\t\n   130\t  | Case | Usable replies |\n   131\t  |---|---|\n   132\t  | P | both writers |\n   133\t  | P-v4 | both writers |\n   134\t  | P-v8 | both writers |\n   135\t  | O | Codex |\n   473\t  settled vote are recorded by Claude as compatible or not (`reasons.json`). Matching votes with incompatible reasons go to\n   474\t  adjudication.\n   475\t\n   476\t  | Control | Built from | Expected reading |\n   477\t  |---|---|---|\n   478\t  | K-invented-cut | blind4 U05, E Codex: 90% of $40B with no order size or shipment pace | not supported |\n   479\t  | K-impossible-floor | blind4 U02, S Claude: \"more than $730B\" called a floor above its own $720B total | \"part above or added into its whole\" on F12, settled; its support is not scored |\n   480\t- **Pass:**\n   481\t  - every fresh S and E reply has a settled yes, on its forecast or on its unresolved reason;\n   482\t  - both fresh P-v8 replies have a settled yes on a forecast;\n   483\t  - every fresh reply is accepted and publishable by the code;\n   484\t  - no settled defect on a fresh reply;\n   485\t  - both controls read as expected.\n   486\t- **Otherwise (tally rules after council 64):**\n   487\t  - **Fail:**\n   488\t    - a fresh reply refused or held by the code (decided before any reading; an unreadable reply builds no packet);\n   489\t    - a fresh P-v8 reply that is not a forecast (decided from its shape alone);\n   490\t    - a settled no on a fresh reply;\n   491\t    - a settled defect on a fresh reply;\n   492\t    - K-invented-cut not settled no;\n   493\t    - the floor defect marked by neither reader, or rejected by both adjudicators.\n   494\t  - **Inconclusive:** a support split, a defect split on a fresh reply, or a floor split left after adjudication.\n   495\t  - **The floor control** is matched on its type and on naming F12, at both stages, so readers who list the other figures differently\n   496\t    still agree. Adjudicator votes are validated like first votes.\n   497\t  - Either outcome goes back to the council, and the paid re-send stays on hold.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind7-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json:83:      \"why\": \"The decisive unknown is how many Vera CPUs customers will take during FY2028 and at what realized price. There is no booked volume, delivery schedule, or reported sales pace to size the gap between evaluations and revenue. Taking the target in full and discounting it into rung 3 would each require an unsupported assumption about that gap.\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind7-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json:188:      \"basis_text\": \"My forecast of FY2028 Vera CPU revenue growth. NVIDIA guides to more than double (F2, a floor of 100% growth), but this is a full-year target for the next fiscal year for a product whose deployments are still ahead, and no order, backlog, unit or price figure puts FY2028 volume in hand, so the target is discounted rather than taken in full. The no-share-gain be\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind7-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json:219:      \"reasons\": \"Reference (rung 2): NVIDIA's own guidance. CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA still expects $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 (F1) and expects FY2028 to more than double it (F2) (e3660); e4691 and e4856 report the same claim as Vera CPU revenue, so the $20 billion is the Vera base. $20 billion x 2 = $40 billion (F3), and 'more than' double places the reference just \n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"agent_message","text":"**Recommend “can’t tell yet” when the evidence leaves both sides open, and keep the paid test on hold.** On the same sales question, one writer said it needed orders or a delivery schedule to choose a range. Another chose 85% growth and forecast $37B. Both readers accepted the first answer and rejected the second because the sources gave no basis for choosing 85%. [Codex answer:83](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind7-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json:83) · [BLIND7-RESULT.md:19](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND7-RESULT.md:19)\n\n1. **The wording rounds ended. The paid test did not earn approval.** D751 specified two outcomes. A clean pass would earn a request for paid authorization. A settled failure would end the wording rounds and return the publication choice to Alex. The final test produced that settled failure. Alex can now choose to waive his earlier condition, but describing a waiver as “the D751 request” gives it authority the decision did not grant. [SMALL-DESIGN.md:580](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:580) · [BLIND7-RESULT.md:27](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND7-RESULT.md:27) · [DECISIONS-log.md:11645](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11645)\n\n   Claude’s account is accurate about the three “can’t tell” answers. Both reader families accepted all three, but all came from the Codex writer on the **same case and passages**. The $42B answer taking guidance in full split the readers. The spending forecast also drew disagreements, including two disputed defects in the preceding round. Both writers consistently produced supported forecasts for the Data Center question, and readers caught all six planted errors across the last three rounds. Those checks establish neither that calculating the two revenue forecasts together helps nor how often Luna would make either mistake. [BLIND7-RESULT.md:30](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND7-RESULT.md:30) · [BLIND5-RESULT.md:42](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND5-RESULT.md:42) · [BLIND6-RESULT.md:48](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND6-RESULT.md:48)\n\n2. **Ask whether the evidence favours a range, rather than whether a source gives the exact size of a miss.** Alex already permits a forecast when a relevant comparison explains why one side is more likely, even if the other remains possible. Guidance standing alone also has its own rule. The open choice concerns a target **at or near a cutoff**, where other evidence bears on the forecast but still leaves the choice open. That wording also fits the actual example more closely. Management expected *more than double* a projected $20B, so $40B was the implied lower edge of its outlook. [DECISIONS-log.md:11575](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11575) · [judging-v13s.txt:4](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt:4) · [E source passages:10](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind7-2026-11-23/E/user.txt:10)\n\n   I recommend **(a)**. Line 9 already tells the writer to say it cannot choose and name the missing fact when the evidence only shows that sales *might* fall short or exceed the target. Alex explicitly endorsed that behaviour. Choosing (a) needs no request edit. The failed test shows that a writer can still disregard the instruction. Choosing (b) would establish a new publication policy. [judging-v13s.txt:9](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt:9) · [DECISIONS-log.md:11645](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11645) · [DECISIONS-log.md:11566](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11566)\n\n3. **Keep the paid test on hold, even though it can still give a meaningful result.** A complete run can pass in exactly two ways: **8 of 9** usable pairs calculated together against **at most 5** calculated separately, or **9 of 9** against **at most 6** separately. Each of the three snapshots needs at least two usable pairs calculated together. If separate calculation gets seven, even a perfect nine cannot pass. One published contradiction, one internal forecast presented as the market’s view, or one unsupported adjustment or scope change that decides the range stops a complete run on any joint or standalone answer. A standalone answer judged unusable does not by itself trigger that stop. Missing required readings or an unsent unit leave the result incomplete. [PAID-GATE.md:259](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:259) · [PAID-GATE.md:270](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:270) · [paid_gate.py:772](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:772)\n\n   The free spending question asked about **2026**. The paid one asks about **2027**. The free disagreement cannot settle that result in advance. A paid run could tell Alex whether this version of Luna benefits from calculating related forecasts together. Even a pass would earn further design work only. That is a reason to leave a one-time exception available to Alex, but the evidence gives me no reason to recommend dropping the condition he expressly chose. [SMALL-DESIGN.md:169](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:169) · [paid_gate.py:44](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:44) · [PAID-GATE.md:278](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:278) · [DECISIONS-log.md:11651](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11651)\n\n4. **The two questions for Alex**\n\n   **Question 1. A company repeats a target near the cutoff between two forecast ranges. After weighing the target and the other evidence, we still can’t say which range is more likely. What should readers see?**\n\n   - **A — recommended.** Show the company’s target as context. Say “We can’t tell yet” and name the fact that would help us choose, such as how much customers have ordered for delivery this year.\n   - **B.** Use the company’s target range as our default forecast. Tell readers that the forecast relies on management meeting its target. [judging-v13s.txt:9](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt:9) · [DECISIONS-log.md:11566](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11566)\n\n   **Question 2. The last free test produced a sales forecast that both readers rejected. Do you want to keep the paid comparison on hold or make a one-time exception?**\n\n   - **A — recommended.** Stop here and spend $0. Keep the agreed first-release plan: readers see the number question, its sources and “Forecast unavailable.”\n   - **B.** Waive the clean-pass condition once. Authorize 36 scored requests to Luna, with any diagnostic retries inside the same **$0.50 total cap**, to see whether calculating related forecasts together gives more usable answers. A pass would justify designing the production change. [DECISIONS-log.md:11004](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11004) · [PAID-GATE.md:21](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:21) · [PAID-GATE.md:35](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:35) · [PAID-GATE.md:278](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:278)"}}
